# philosophy.kb — the branches of philosophy, the schools, the arguments, and the world religions.
#
# society.kb names `philosophy`, `ethics`, `epistemology`, `metaphysics` and six religions in one
# line each. This file is the structure behind them, and it is written with one restraint that
# matters more here than in physics: **a claim about what a tradition holds is not a claim that
# the tradition is right.** Every religious line below is written as "this is what is taught",
# which a fact store can hold honestly; nothing here asserts a contested metaphysical claim as
# fact, because the store has no way to mark one as disputed and she would state it flatly.
#
# The same restraint is why the ethical theories carry `means` (what the theory says) and never
# `causes` (what following it produces).

# --- the branches ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ #
philosophy | means=the study of the most general questions about knowledge, being, value and reason | has_kind=aesthetics | has_kind=political philosophy
metaphysics | means=the study of what there is and what it is to be | involves=identity | involves=time
epistemology | means=the study of knowledge and how it is justified | involves=belief | involves=truth | involves=doubt
ethics | means=the study of what one ought to do | has_kind=normative ethics | has_kind=metaethics | has_kind=applied ethics
logic | is_a=branch of philosophy | means=the study of what follows from what | has_kind=deductive logic | has_kind=inductive logic | purpose=telling a valid argument from an invalid one
political philosophy | is_a=branch of philosophy | means=the study of authority, justice and the state | involves=legitimacy | involves=liberty | involves=justice
philosophy of mind | is_a=branch of philosophy | means=the study of what the mind is and how it relates to the body | involves=consciousness | involves=intention
philosophy of science | is_a=branch of philosophy | means=the study of what makes an inquiry scientific | involves=explanation | involves=evidence | involves=falsifiability

# --- the vocabulary of argument -------------------------------------------------------------- #
argument | is_a=structure of reasoning | has_part=premise | has_part=conclusion | purpose=giving a reason to accept a claim
premise | is_a=part of an argument | means=a statement offered in support of the conclusion
conclusion | is_a=part of an argument | means=the statement an argument is offered in support of
validity | is_a=property of an argument | means=that the conclusion cannot be false while the premises are true | has_property=about form rather than content
soundness | is_a=property of an argument | means=valid and with true premises | requires=validity
deduction | is_a=form of inference | means=reasoning in which the conclusion follows necessarily from the premises | has_property=truth preserving
induction | is_a=form of inference | means=reasoning from observed cases to a general claim | has_property=defeasible@0.9
abduction | is_a=form of inference | means=reasoning to the explanation that would best account for what is observed | also_known_as=inference to the best explanation
syllogism | is_a=deductive argument | has_property=two premises and a conclusion | discoverer=Aristotle
fallacy | is_a=defect in reasoning | means=a pattern of argument that looks to support its conclusion and does not | has_kind=circular reasoning | has_kind=false dilemma | has_kind=ad hominem | has_kind=straw man
circular reasoning | is_a=fallacy | means=assuming in the premises what the conclusion is supposed to establish
false dilemma | is_a=fallacy | means=presenting two options as exhaustive when they are not
ad hominem | is_a=fallacy | means=attacking the person rather than the argument
straw man | is_a=fallacy | means=refuting a weakened version of the opposing view
thought experiment | is_a=method | purpose=testing a theory by imagining a case rather than running one | involves=imagination
Occam's razor | is_a=principle | means=that of two explanations that fit the evidence the simpler is to be preferred | has_property=a guide rather than a proof
burden of proof | is_a=principle | means=that whoever asserts a claim owes the reason for it
paradox | is_a=argument | means=an apparently sound argument reaching an unacceptable conclusion | has_kind=Ship of Theseus | has_kind=liar paradox
liar paradox | is_a=paradox | involves=a sentence that says of itself that it is false
Ship of Theseus | is_a=paradox | involves=whether a thing whose parts are all replaced is the same thing

# --- epistemology and ethics in detail ------------------------------------------------------------- #
knowledge | is_a=mental state | means=a belief that is both true and held for a good reason | requires=belief | requires=truth | requires=justification
belief | is_a=mental state | means=taking a claim to be true | has_property=can be false
justification | is_a=property of a belief | means=the reason there is to hold it
scepticism | is_a=philosophical position | means=the view that we know less than we suppose | involves=doubt
empiricism | is_a=philosophical position | means=the view that knowledge comes from experience | involves=observation
rationalism | is_a=philosophical position | means=the view that reason can reach truths experience cannot
a priori | is_a=kind of knowledge | means=knowable without appeal to experience
a posteriori | is_a=kind of knowledge | means=knowable only through experience
consequentialism | is_a=ethical theory | means=the view that an act is right in virtue of its consequences | has_kind=utilitarianism
utilitarianism | is_a=consequentialism | means=the view that the right act is the one producing the most well-being overall
deontology | is_a=ethical theory | means=the view that some acts are right or wrong in themselves, whatever they produce | involves=duty
virtue ethics | is_a=ethical theory | means=the view that ethics is about what kind of person to be rather than which act to do | involves=character | discoverer=Aristotle
moral relativism | is_a=ethical position | means=the view that moral claims hold only relative to a culture or a person
golden rule | is_a=moral principle | means=treat others as you would have them treat you | has_property=stated in many traditions independently
justice | is_a=value | means=each being given what is due to them | has_kind=distributive justice | has_kind=retributive justice
free will | is_a=philosophical problem | involves=whether a choice could have been made otherwise | involves=determinism
determinism | is_a=philosophical position | means=the view that every event is fixed by what came before it
consciousness | is_a=philosophical problem | means=there being something it is like to be a thing | involves=experience | involves=the mind
dualism | is_a=philosophical position | means=the view that mind and body are two kinds of thing
materialism | is_a=philosophical position | means=the view that everything that exists is physical

# --- philosophers ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
Socrates | birthplace=Athens | is_a=teacher of Plato | involves=questioning as a method
Plato | is_a=philosopher | birthplace=Athens | author=Republic of Plato | is_a=student of Socrates
Aristotle | birthplace=Stagira | is_a=student of Plato | is_a=founder of formal logic
Confucius | birthplace=China | involves=duty within relationships | involves=ritual propriety
Immanuel Kant | is_a=philosopher | birthplace=Konigsberg | involves=duty | involves=the limits of reason
Rene Descartes | is_a=philosopher | birthplace=France | involves=methodical doubt | involves=dualism
David Hume | is_a=philosopher | birthplace=Edinburgh | involves=empiricism | involves=the problem of induction
John Stuart Mill | is_a=philosopher | birthplace=London | involves=utilitarianism | involves=liberty
Adi Shankara | is_a=philosopher | birthplace=India | involves=Advaita Vedanta
Republic of Plato | is_a=philosophical work | author=Plato | involves=justice | involves=the ideal state

# --- religion, described rather than asserted -------------------------------------------------------- #
religion | is_a=cultural institution | involves=belief about the sacred | involves=a community | involves=a moral teaching
scripture | is_a=text | means=a text a religious tradition treats as authoritative
ritual | is_a=practice | means=an act performed in a set form for its meaning rather than its effect
prayer | is_a=religious practice | purpose=addressing the divine
pilgrimage | is_a=religious practice | means=a journey undertaken to a place held sacred
place of worship | is_a=building | purpose=gathering a religious community | has_kind=temple | has_kind=church | has_kind=mosque | has_kind=synagogue | has_kind=gurdwara
monotheism | is_a=religious position | means=the belief that there is one God
polytheism | is_a=religious position | means=the belief that there are many gods
atheism | is_a=position on religion | means=the absence of belief that any god exists
agnosticism | is_a=position on religion | means=the view that whether a god exists is not known
secularism | is_a=political principle | means=that the state does not favour a religion
theology | is_a=discipline | means=the systematic study of a religion's own doctrine
Christianity | involves=the teachings of Jesus | has_kind=Catholicism | has_kind=Protestantism | has_kind=Orthodox Christianity
Bible | is_a=scripture | has_part=Old Testament | has_part=New Testament
church | is_a=place of worship | purpose=Christian worship
Islam | involves=the teachings of Muhammad | involves=the five pillars | has_kind=Sunni Islam | has_kind=Shia Islam
Quran | is_a=scripture | purpose=the scripture of Islam
mosque | is_a=place of worship | purpose=Islamic worship
Hinduism | birthplace=India | involves=dharma | involves=karma | has_property=many traditions rather than one founder
Vedas | is_a=scripture | located_in=India | has_property=among the oldest religious texts
Bhagavad Gita | is_a=scripture | part_of=Mahabharata | involves=duty | involves=action without attachment
temple | is_a=place of worship | purpose=worship in Hindu, Buddhist, Jain and other traditions
dharma | is_a=religious concept | means=the duty or right way of living a tradition sets out
karma | is_a=religious concept | means=the teaching that an act has consequences for the one who does it
Buddhism | birthplace=India | involves=the four noble truths | involves=the eightfold path | involves=the teachings of the Buddha
Buddha | is_a=religious teacher | also_known_as=Siddhartha Gautama | birthplace=Lumbini
four noble truths | is_a=religious teaching | part_of=Buddhism | involves=suffering | involves=the end of suffering
Judaism | involves=a covenant | has_property=among the oldest monotheistic religions
Torah | is_a=scripture | purpose=the first section of the Hebrew Bible
synagogue | is_a=place of worship | purpose=Jewish worship
Sikhism | birthplace=Punjab | has_part=Guru Granth Sahib | involves=the teachings of Guru Nanak | involves=one God
Guru Granth Sahib | is_a=scripture | purpose=the scripture of Sikhism
gurdwara | is_a=place of worship | purpose=Sikh worship | has_part=langar
Jainism | is_a=religion | birthplace=India | involves=non-violence | involves=asceticism
Taoism | is_a=religion | birthplace=China | involves=living in accordance with the Tao
Shinto | is_a=religion | birthplace=Japan | involves=kami
meditation | is_a=practice | purpose=training attention | causes=calm@0.8 | involves=sustained attention
fasting | is_a=practice | means=abstaining from food for a set period | purpose=religious observance or health
festival | purpose=marking a date a community holds significant | has_kind=Diwali | has_kind=Eid | has_kind=Christmas | has_kind=Holi | has_kind=Hanukkah | has_kind=Vaisakhi
Diwali | is_a=festival | located_in=India | involves=lamps | means=the festival of lights
Eid al-Fitr | is_a=festival | occurs_when=the month of Ramadan ends | involves=a communal prayer
Christmas | is_a=festival | involves=the birth of Jesus | has_property=celebrated on the twenty-fifth of December
Holi | is_a=festival | located_in=India | involves=coloured powder | occurs_when=spring arrives
Ramadan | is_a=month | part_of=the Islamic calendar | involves=fasting from dawn to sunset
